---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';

/**
 * Four things are mine to build, and each section is one of them.
 *
 * Two ledgers were cut rather than shortened. `loop` gave the page a five-stage
 * process running alongside a four-part spine — two spines for one argument.
 * `encode` listed design intent, tokens, components and behavioural rules
 * producing the interface, which is the sentence above it, drawn.
 */

/** The brief, and the question each slot answers. */
const brief = [
  ['Intent', 'Who is trying to do what, and which decision should get easier?'],
  ['Required behavior', 'What has to remain true across every state, transition, permission and failure path?'],
  ['System constraints', 'Which tokens, components and patterns must the work resolve to?'],
  ['Prohibited actions', 'What may the agent not do without a person?'],
  ['Open questions', 'What should the work help us resolve?'],
  ['Evaluation criteria', 'What would make this wrong even if it looked finished?'],
];

/** Six lenses, one question each. */
const lenses = [
  ['Intent', 'Does this solve the problem we agreed to solve?'],
  ['Behavior', 'Do the states, transitions, permissions and failure paths make sense?'],
  ['System', 'Does this strengthen the product’s patterns, or erode them?'],
  ['Content', 'Does the interface explain what happened and what to do next?'],
  ['Accessibility', 'Can people perceive, understand and operate it in real conditions?'],
  ['Implementation', 'Is the behavior credible in the actual product environment?'],
];

/**
 * Only the boundaries a design leader sets. "Do not deploy to production" and
 * "do not delete data" were here too; anyone who has run an agent for a week
 * sets those.
 */
const boundaries = [
  ['Invent design-system values', 'If the system has no approved value, the agent names the gap. It does not fill it.'],
  ['Resolve product ambiguity', 'An assumption made at speed is still an assumption, and it arrives looking finished.'],
  ['Treat valid as approved', 'Passing every check is not the same as being the right thing to build.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I build the system that AI designs within"
  metaTitle="I build the system that AI designs within | Tyler Young"
  description="An agent can build a screen in minutes. Knowing whether it is the right screen still takes a person who understands the problem. The design systems, constraints and review loops I build for agents to work inside."
  eyebrow="How I design with AI"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Frame · direct · evaluate · align'],
    ['Output', 'Working options, production-ready systems, clearer decisions'],
    ['Principle', 'The machine increases range. People remain responsible for meaning and quality.'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    An agent can build a screen in minutes. Knowing whether it is the right
    screen still takes a person who understands the problem. I build what agents
    work inside, and I am accountable for what ships.
  </p>

  <p>
    Turning an idea into something a team could use once took weeks. Now I build
    several working directions in hours and let the team compare real behavior
    instead of imagined outcomes. The work moves faster; my responsibility does
    not change.
  </p>
  <p>
    What changed is where my decisions land. A rule I set — this state has to
    exist, this colour comes from the token page, this action needs a person —
    once got written down and checked later by whoever built it. Now it is
    applied while the thing is being built. So the work moved upstream of the
    screen, into four things that are mine to build.
  </p>

  <h2>The brief</h2>
  <p>
    An agent will build a polished version of a poorly framed idea, so I write
    the brief first. It answers six questions.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      brief.map(([label, question]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{question}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>That is more specification than a screen drawing carries.</p>

  <h2>The system</h2>
  <p>
    Every colour, type style, spacing value, radius, pattern and accessibility
    rule resolves to the design system. Where a value does not exist, the agent
    names the gap instead of inventing one. That makes the system part of the
    environment the interface comes out of, rather than a document someone
    consults afterwards.
  </p>
  <p>
    At Atheer I rebuilt an ageing system into a pipeline that worked this way.
    Explorations came back as working components, designers could explore
    without breaking coherence, engineers got behaviour as code, and
    accessibility and brand rules arrived before review instead of after it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/ops-manager">The product and the system it was built from</a> are both
    running, and the component library documents the states that prove it.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    A design system this close to production decides what the product is allowed
    to become.
  </p>

  <h2>The loop</h2>
  <p>
    Making three versions is cheap now; choosing between them still costs. On a
    recent engagement I built three approaches to the same problem and deployed
    each to its own URL, so the team judged behavior, content and edge cases
    instead of debating static pictures.
  </p>
  <p>
    Running software also surfaces what a specification hides — a designer and
    an engineer using the same word differently, an agent satisfying the
    sentence and missing the reason behind it. I still read every option, rank
    them, and say why.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/review-loop">A stakeholder review run this way</a> is on the site: two
    running builds, forty-eight notes, and the three that show where the human
    work actually was.
  </p>
  <p>
    A generated screen can be clean, consistent, and still wrong, so I read it
    against six questions.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      lenses.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    I do not need to draw every artifact by hand to hold a bar. I do need to see
    where the work is coherent, where it is merely plausible, and where the
    system underneath it has to improve.
  </p>

  <h2>The perimeter</h2>
  <p>
    An agent can run checks, implement approved patterns, generate options
    inside the system, and propose an addition when no pattern fits. Three
    limits are worth stating, and they are the places where it would otherwise
    make a design decision by default.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      boundaries.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    Speed magnifies misunderstanding, so anything that ships still belongs to a
    person. Whoever approves the work needs to know what changed, which checks
    ran, and what could be affected. In
    <a class="link" href="/alucify">Alucify</a> I designed the surfaces that make exactly that
    inspectable.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    I automate execution more readily than I automate judgment.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed is the material</h2>
  <p>
    I am not a frontend engineer and I do not think designers need to become
    one. Design leadership used to scale through capacity — how much a team
    could research, draw, review and shepherd through implementation — and the
    four things above are the other constraint now.
  </p>
  <p>
    Less gets lost between the people who decide something and the people who
    build it. The tools are new; what the work has to be has not changed:
    useful, coherent, accessible, explainable, and right for the people it
    serves.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    My leverage is the system the work comes out of.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
